From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 13:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 207/207] win32u: iOS: a cache DC never releases another
 process's window surface
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On iOS every Wine process is a pseudo-process of one app, so win32u's
list of DCEs is shared by all of them, and NtUserGetDCEx handed a
process the cache DCs of others, including those of a process that
had ended. A released cache DC keeps the surface of the window it was
last taken for until it is taken again, and the process that takes
it releases that surface. When that was the last reference, the
surface's pixels were freed with NtFreeVirtualMemory in the taker's
own address space.

Borderlands 2's Steam prerequisites showed what that costs (PLA-871).
The pixels of a 32-bit process's window are in its WoW64 window. The
VC++ 2005 installer's inner process had its dialog's pixels at host
0x71c0f90000, and its window slot was then given to the VC++ 2005 ATL
installer's inner process, whose dialog's pixels were allocated at the
same address. Painting its dialog, that process took a DC the first
one left, dropped the old surface, and freed its own pixels: the view
became a window gap, and the erase fault in solid_rects_32 could not
be dispatched. The process ended with 0x45 holding its surface's lock
(PLA-870), and VC++ 2005 ATL was recorded as installed although it was
not.

A DC now records the process that took it last, keyed on the pid and
PEB as the builtin classes are (a reused pid alone does not match):
the window surface it can hold is that process's. The cache stays one
list of 64 DCs in all, as before, but a process takes another
process's unused DC only when it cannot hold a live surface of that
process: an empty DC, or one whose window went with its thread or
process, which never ran free_dce for it. Taking one, it forgets the
surface the DC may still hold (dibdrv_forget_window_surface: the DC
gets the dummy surface, and the old reference is left, never
released) instead of releasing it, so no surface is ever released
outside its process. A DC on a live window of another process is
never taken; when the full list has only such DCs, the caller makes a
new one and says so on its first eight times ("[dce] pid …: a full DC
cache, N DCs of other processes not taken"), so a phone log shows the
case PLA-871 was.

A released cache DC is disabled, and set_visible_region does nothing
to a disabled DC, so release_dce (DestroyWindow's free_dce) left the
window's surface in it. The process destroying its window now enables
its own DC for that call, which releases the surface there: an empty
DC holds none, and a later taker has nothing to forget.

Class: ios-port
Evidence: PLA-871 (the phone's log of IPA 0023f811: the committed 0x60000 allocation at guest 0xfa0000 of the ATL installer's process is a window-gap view coalesced with its left neighbour when it faults, the same address and size the VC++ 2005 installer's process had in the same slot); tools/tests/test_dce_cache_ios.py (alloc_dce, release_dce, free_dce, the helpers and this search, with a DC model that fails a surface released outside its process: a hundred processes that each fill the cache and then die or destroy their windows leave one list of 64 DCs; a full cache of another process's DCs on its live windows gets the next process a new DC; an ended process's DC is taken with its surface forgotten; a process reuses its own DCs)
Offered-upstream: no; iOS one-process runtime integration
---
 dlls/win32u/dce.c           | 104 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 dlls/win32u/dibdrv/dc.c     |  19 +++++++
 dlls/win32u/ntgdi_private.h |   3 ++
 3 files changed, 126 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/win32u/dce.c b/dlls/win32u/dce.c
index 2627c72..bfdc355 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/dce.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/dce.c
@@ -43,6 +43,10 @@ struct dce
     UINT        flags;
     LONG        count;         /* usage count; 0 or 1 for cache DCEs, always 1 for window DCEs,
                                   always >= 1 for class DCEs */
+#ifdef WINE_IOS
+    DWORD       pid;           /* the pseudo-process that took it last, and its PEB */
+    void       *peb;
+#endif
 };
 
 static struct list dce_list = LIST_INIT(dce_list);
@@ -908,14 +912,78 @@ static void update_visible_region( struct dce *dce )
     if (surface) window_surface_release( surface );
 }
 
+#ifdef WINE_IOS
+/* Playport (PLA-871): every pseudo-process of the app shares dce_list, which on desktop
+ * holds only the calling process's DCs. A cache DC keeps the surface of the window it was
+ * last taken for, and the process that takes it next releases that surface. When that was
+ * the last reference, the release freed the surface's pixels with NtFreeVirtualMemory in
+ * the taker's own address space: the pixels of a 32-bit process were in its WoW64 window,
+ * and when the next 32-bit process's window reused that slot, the address was the new
+ * process's own window surface (the VC++ 2005 ATL installer then faulted painting its
+ * dialog). A DC records the process that took it last, keyed on (pid, PEB) as the builtin
+ * classes are, so that a reused pid alone does not match: the surface it can hold is that
+ * process's. */
+static BOOL is_current_process_dce( const struct dce *dce )
+{
+    return dce->pid == HandleToULong( NtCurrentTeb()->ClientId.UniqueProcess ) &&
+           dce->peb == NtCurrentTeb()->Peb;
+}
+
+/* Whether an unused cache DC another process took last is on a live window of that
+ * process, and so may hold that window's surface: such a DC is never taken. An empty DC
+ * holds none, and the window of any other went with its thread or process, which never
+ * ran free_dce for it. */
+static BOOL is_live_other_process_dce( const struct dce *dce )
+{
+    DWORD error = RtlGetLastWin32Error(), pid;
+    BOOL ret = dce->hwnd && get_window_thread( dce->hwnd, &pid ) && pid == dce->pid;
+
+    RtlSetLastWin32Error( error );  /* get_window_thread sets it for a window that is gone */
+    return ret;
+}
+
+/* Make a cache DC another process took last the caller's, with the DC enabled. A surface
+ * it still holds is that process's: the DC forgets it rather than release it, since its
+ * last release would free the pixels in the caller's address space; they went, or go,
+ * with their own process. A DC another thread holds cannot be reached, by the
+ * set_visible_region of the caller either. */
+static void take_other_process_dce( struct dce *dce )
+{
+    DC *dc = get_dc_ptr( dce->hdc );
+
+    if (dc)
+    {
+        dibdrv_forget_window_surface( dc );
+        release_dc_ptr( dc );
+    }
+    dce->pid = HandleToULong( NtCurrentTeb()->ClientId.UniqueProcess );
+    dce->peb = NtCurrentTeb()->Peb;
+}
+#endif
+
 /***********************************************************************
  *           release_dce
  */
 static void release_dce( struct dce *dce )
 {
+#ifdef WINE_IOS
+    BOOL disabled;
+#endif
+
     if (!dce->hwnd) return;  /* already released */
 
+#ifdef WINE_IOS
+    /* Playport (PLA-871): an unused cache DC is disabled, and set_visible_region does
+     * nothing to a disabled DC, so the DC kept its window's surface after the window was
+     * gone, until some process took the DC again. Its own process (the one destroying the
+     * window) enables it for the call and releases the surface here: an empty DC holds
+     * none, whichever process takes it next. */
+    disabled = is_current_process_dce( dce ) && set_dce_flags( dce->hdc, DCHF_ENABLEDC );
+#endif
     set_visible_region( dce->hdc, 0, &dummy_surface.rect, &dummy_surface.rect, &dummy_surface );
+#ifdef WINE_IOS
+    if (disabled) set_dce_flags( dce->hdc, DCHF_DISABLEDC );
+#endif
     user_driver->pReleaseDC( dce->hwnd, dce->hdc );
 
     if (dce->clip_rgn) NtGdiDeleteObjectApp( dce->clip_rgn );
@@ -1002,6 +1070,10 @@ static struct dce *alloc_dce(void)
     dce->clip_rgn  = 0;
     dce->flags     = 0;
     dce->count     = 1;
+#ifdef WINE_IOS
+    dce->pid       = HandleToULong( NtCurrentTeb()->ClientId.UniqueProcess );
+    dce->peb       = NtCurrentTeb()->Peb;
+#endif
 
     set_dc_dce( dce->hdc, dce );
     return dce;
@@ -1343,6 +1415,10 @@ HDC WINAPI NtUserGetDCEx( HWND hwnd, HRGN clip_rgn, DWORD flags )
     {
         struct dce *dceEmpty = NULL, *dceUnused = NULL, *found = NULL;
         unsigned int count = 0;
+#ifdef WINE_IOS
+        struct dce *other_unused = NULL;
+        unsigned int others = 0;
+#endif
 
         /* Strategy: First, we attempt to find a non-empty but unused DCE with
          * compatible flags. Next, we look for an empty entry. If the cache is
@@ -1354,6 +1430,18 @@ HDC WINAPI NtUserGetDCEx( HWND hwnd, HRGN clip_rgn, DWORD flags )
             if (!(dce->flags & DCX_CACHE)) break;
             count++;
             if (dce->count) continue;
+#ifdef WINE_IOS
+            if (!is_current_process_dce( dce ))  /* PLA-871 */
+            {
+                if (!is_live_other_process_dce( dce )) dceEmpty = dce;
+                else
+                {
+                    others++;
+                    other_unused = dce;
+                }
+                continue;
+            }
+#endif
             dceUnused = dce;
             if (!dce->hwnd) dceEmpty = dce;
             else if ((dce->hwnd == hwnd) && !((dce->flags ^ flags) & clip_flags))
@@ -1366,12 +1454,28 @@ HDC WINAPI NtUserGetDCEx( HWND hwnd, HRGN clip_rgn, DWORD flags )
         }
         if (!found) found = dceEmpty;
         if (!found && count >= DCE_CACHE_SIZE) found = dceUnused;
+#ifdef WINE_IOS
+        /* the case PLA-871 was: the shared list full, with unused DCs of other processes on
+         * their live windows in it */
+        if (!found && other_unused && count >= DCE_CACHE_SIZE)
+        {
+            static LONG logged;
+            if (InterlockedIncrement( &logged ) <= 8)
+                fprintf( stderr, "[dce] pid %04x: a full DC cache, %u DCs of other processes not taken "
+                         "(the oldest unused: pid %04x, hwnd %p)\n",
+                         (UINT)HandleToULong( NtCurrentTeb()->ClientId.UniqueProcess ), others,
+                         (UINT)other_unused->pid, other_unused->hwnd );
+        }
+#endif
 
         dce = found;
         if (dce)
         {
             dce->count = 1;
             set_dce_flags( dce->hdc, DCHF_ENABLEDC );
+#ifdef WINE_IOS
+            if (!is_current_process_dce( dce )) take_other_process_dce( dce );
+#endif
         }
         user_unlock();
 
diff --git a/dlls/win32u/dibdrv/dc.c b/dlls/win32u/dibdrv/dc.c
index 8a72d97..d0afa31 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/dibdrv/dc.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/dibdrv/dc.c
@@ -610,6 +610,25 @@ void dibdrv_set_window_surface( DC *dc, struct window_surface *surface )
     }
 }
 
+#ifdef WINE_IOS
+/* Playport (PLA-871): the DC gives up its window surface for the dummy one without
+ * releasing it. The caller takes over a cache DC another process took last: the surface
+ * is that process's, and its last release would free its pixels with NtFreeVirtualMemory
+ * in the caller's address space. The reference is left, so the surface is never freed. */
+void dibdrv_forget_window_surface( DC *dc )
+{
+    PHYSDEV windev = find_dc_driver( dc, &window_driver );
+    struct windrv_physdev *physdev;
+
+    if (!windev) return;
+    physdev = get_windrv_physdev( windev );
+    if (physdev->surface == &dummy_surface) return;
+    window_surface_add_ref( &dummy_surface );
+    physdev->surface = &dummy_surface;
+    dibdrv_set_window_surface( dc, &dummy_surface );  /* and draws to the dummy surface's bits */
+}
+#endif
+
 static BOOL windrv_AlphaBlend( PHYSDEV dst_dev, struct bitblt_coords *dst,
                                PHYSDEV src_dev, struct bitblt_coords *src, BLENDFUNCTION func )
 {
diff --git a/dlls/win32u/ntgdi_private.h b/dlls/win32u/ntgdi_private.h
index fcfbce5..9c4e4e5 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/ntgdi_private.h
+++ b/dlls/win32u/ntgdi_private.h
@@ -223,6 +223,9 @@ extern UINT get_dib_dc_color_table( HDC hdc, UINT startpos, UINT entries,
 extern UINT set_dib_dc_color_table( HDC hdc, UINT startpos, UINT entries,
                                     const RGBQUAD *colors );
 extern void dibdrv_set_window_surface( DC *dc, struct window_surface *surface );
+#ifdef WINE_IOS
+extern void dibdrv_forget_window_surface( DC *dc );
+#endif
 
 /* driver.c */
 extern const struct gdi_dc_funcs null_driver;
